    issue  arch    mirror dir          artifacts  signature (harness line)
    #425   arm64   3302a46a4cd70ab5      5,168    differential_dav1d.rs:130  10/12-bit plane compare
    #426   x86_64  5464037781fd2a2a          1    differential_dav1d.rs:99   8-bit plane compare
    #433   arm64   2271c8fb62186ea5          1    differential_dav1d.rs:192  rav1d decoded, dav1d errored

Everything below is output from a command run on this box. Nothing is estimated.


## 1. Conformance: aomdec rejects all 5,170

Each artifact wrapped as a one-frame IVF (`~/tmp/ivfwrap.py`) and fed to aomdec
(`--summary -o /dev/null`); the first diagnostic line tallied:

    "Failed to decode frame 1: Bitstream not supported by this decoder"   4,198
    "Failed to decode frame 1: Corrupt frame detected"                        972
    decoded a frame                                                             0

    #425 pile: 4,196 unsupported + 972 corrupt.   #426: unsupported.   #433: unsupported.

dav1d 1.5.3 CLI on the three artifacts the issues name:

    #426  --strict 0: Decoded 1/1 frames      --strict 1: No data decoded
    #425  --strict 0: Decoded 1/1 frames      --strict 1: No data decoded
    #433  --strict 0: No data decoded         --strict 1: No data decoded
          (matches its signature: rav1d-safe decoded a frame, dav1d errored)

So every one of the 5,170 inputs is a stream the reference decoder refuses, and
everything the harness compared on them is concealment of data the spec does
not define.


## 2. What diverged, lenient against lenient (the target as it was)

The x86_64 build of the old target (both decoders at their lenient defaults),
one run per artifact, before the target was rebuilt strict (§3):

    #426  x86_64 artifact:  panicked at differential_dav1d.rs:103
          DIVERGENCE Y row 0 col 5: rav1d=54 dav1d=55
          -> a genuine, arch-independent one-level difference in how the two
             decoders conceal this 37-byte stream.
    #433  arm64 artifact:   no divergence on x86_64 (rc 0)
          -> the accept/reject asymmetry the farm saw is aarch64-specific.
    #425  first 1,292 of 5,168 arm64 artifacts: no divergence on x86_64 (rc 0)
          -> the 10-bit divergence class is aarch64-specific too (the rebuild in
             §3 replaced the binary mid-sweep; the remaining 3,876 were not run
             lenient on x86).

No attempt was made to make the two concealments agree: the spec leaves the
decode of these streams undefined, dav1d's lenient path is not a specification,
and the production default is now Strict (strictness_2026-08-28.meta).


## 3. Strict against strict (the target as it is now)

`fuzz/fuzz_targets/differential_dav1d.rs` now sets `Strictness::Strict` on
rav1d-safe and `set_strict_std_compliance(true)` on dav1d. Same 5,170 inputs,
one run each:

    #425  5,168 / 5,168   rc 0   (both reject; symmetric)
    #426      1 / 1       rc 0
    #433      1 / 1       rc 0

An accept/reject asymmetry under this configuration would be a real finding —
a conformance check one decoder lacks — and still panics the target. The three
issues are closed against this record; the committed seed
fuzz/regression/differential_dav1d/crash-itx-16x64-dc-rect2 (the #400 class)
keeps its reach through the lenient decode_obu / parse_seq_header entry points
of tests/fuzz_regression.rs.
